Revised analysis on the comparison between experiments and model outputs;
Added computational analysis characterizing the learning dynamics in the models;
Added supplementary analysis;
Discussed related works

sec.p:
We assume that the evaluation of song generation and learning happen at the syllable level. For every syllable index (“state”) s(t), the actor generates a set of weights a(s) used to linearly combine a set of spectrogram basis elements stored in the rows of a matrix B. That is, the predicted spectrogram is a(s)⊤B (Fig. 6A). As above, the generated spectrograms undergo a sparse linear embedding, forming the auditory population input to our local circuit model. Rather than maximizing external rewards from the environment, our agent is trained to minimize the population averaged error signal at each time step, equivalent to maximizing an internal reward signal equal to the negative of this quantity: R(s)∝−r¯E,s\begin{document}$R(s) \propto-\bar{r}_{E, s}$\end{document}, where rE\begin{document}$r_{E}$\end{document} is the mean response of the excitatory population. The agent’s actions are parameterized as a state-dependent Gaussian distribution over basis elements: a(s) ∼ 𝒩(µ(s), I), and at each step of learning, the temporal difference (TD) error signal δ is used to update the agent’s estimate of the value of each state V (s). Specifically, after each syllable, these functions are updated by a standard temporal difference learning algorithm:
(1)
δ=−cr‾E,s−V(s)(TDerror)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \delta = - c \bar{r}_{E,s} - V(s) \qquad(\mathrm{TD \, error})$$\end{document}
(2)
μ(s)←μ(s)+αδ(a(s)−μ(s)) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \boldsymbol{\mu}(s) \leftarrow \boldsymbol{\mu}(s)+\alpha \delta(\mathbf{a}(s)-\boldsymbol{\mu}(s)) \tag{2} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(3)
V(s)←V(s)+αδ, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} V(s) \leftarrow V(s)+\alpha \delta, \tag{3} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where α is the learning rate and c = 10 is a constant scaling the mean rate.

The vocal error circuit models can be described by a set of differential equations for the total input ha∈RNa\begin{document}$\mathbf{h}^{a} \in \mathbb{R}^{N_{a}}$\end{document} (where a = E, I)
(4)
τEddthE=−hE+WErH+JEErE−JEIrI+y+ϵtE \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \tau_{E} \frac{\mathrm{~d}}{\mathrm{~d} t} \mathbf{h}^{E}=-\mathbf{h}^{E}+W_{E} \mathbf{r}^{H}+J^{E E} \mathbf{r}^{E}-J^{E I} \mathbf{r}^{I}+\mathbf{y}+\boldsymbol{\epsilon}_{t}^{E} \tag{4} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(5)
τIddthI=−hI+JIErE−JIIrI+ϵtI \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \tau_{I} \frac{\mathrm{~d}}{\mathrm{~d} t} \mathbf{h}^{I}=-\mathbf{h}^{I}+J^{I E} \mathbf{r}^{E}-J^{I I} \mathbf{r}^{I}+\boldsymbol{\epsilon}_{t}^{I} \tag{5} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where rE = ϕE (hE), rI = ϕE (hI) and rH∈RNH\begin{document}$\mathbf{r}^{H} \in \mathbb{R}^{N_{H}}$\end{document} denote the excitatory, inhibitory, and premotor neuron firing rates, respectively, the τ ‘s are time constants, and
ϕb(x)=rmax2[1+erf(x−θbsb2)] \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \phi_{b}(x)=\frac{r_{\max }}{2}\left[1+\operatorname{erf}\left(\frac{x-\theta_{b}}{s_{b} \sqrt{2}}\right)\right]$$\end{document}
are sigmoidal activation functions. Values of the parameters for the main results are summarized in Table 1. Reasonable choices of maximum firing rates (rmaxI≥rmaxE)\begin{document}$\left(r_{\max }^{I} \geq r_{\max }^{E}\right)$\end{document} did not qualitatively affect the dynamics. WE are the synaptic weights from premotor input to the excitatory neurons, Jab ≥ 0 are local synaptic connections from population b to population a, y is the auditory input, and ϵta∼N(0,σϵ)\begin{document}$\epsilon_{t}^{a} \sim \mathcal{N}\left(0, \sigma_{\epsilon}\right)$\end{document} is the i.i.d. white noise at each time step.

In the premotor →E and E→E models, anti-Hebbian learning happens in either premotor →E or E→E connections. In the E→I→E model, Hebbian learning happens in E→I and I→E connections. Both learning rules can be described by the same differential equation, where the learning strength η is strictly positive for Hebbian learning, and strictly negative for anti-Hebbian learning.


τWddtWij=−(Wij−w0)+η[ri(t)−θactivei][rj(t−Δt)−θactivej]
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \tau_{W} \frac{\mathrm{~d}}{\mathrm{~d} t} W_{i j} & =-\left(W_{i j}-w_{0}\right) \\ & +\eta\left[r_{i}(t)-\theta_{\text {active }}^{i}\right]\left[r_{j}\left(t-\Delta_{t}\right)-\theta_{\text {active }}^{j}\right]\end{aligned}$$\end{document}



where Wij is the synaptic weight from neuron j to neuron i, τW ≫ 0 is the time constant of the weight evolution, w0 is the weight baseline, Δt is the time asymmetry of the plasticity, and θactive ∼O(1) are thresholds for determining whether the pre- or postsynaptic neurons are active. The first term is used to prevent the weights from growing to infinity. The second term learns the correlation or anti-correlation between the pre- and postsynaptic neurons. Briefly, in (anti-)Hebbian learning, the synaptic weight is increased (decreased) when both the pre- and postsynaptic neurons are active, and decreased (increased) when they are not co-active. To obey Dale’s law, the weights are clipped at each time step:


Wij←max{Wij,0}
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle W_{i j} \leftarrow \max \left\{W_{i j}, 0\right\}$$\end{document}



Values of the parameters are summarized in Table 1.

We assume that premotor neurons in our model respond to tutor song, as has been found for HVC (35–38, 40). Specifically, the firing rate of a premotor neuron riH\begin{document}$r_{i}^{H}$\end{document} for i = 1, 2, …, NH in multiple renditions j = 1, 2, …, Nrend is modeled as
riH(t)=∑jrburst(j)exp⁡[(t−t(j)−δburst(j))22(τburst(j))2][7] \begin{document}$$\displaystyle r_{i}^{H}(t)=\sum_{j} r_{\mathrm{burst}}^{(j)} \exp \left[\frac{\left(t-t^{(j)}-\delta_{\mathrm{burst}}^{(j)}\right)^{2}}{2\left(\tau_{\mathrm{burst}}^{(j)}\right)^{2}}\right] \quad[\mathbf{7}]$$\end{document}
where rburst(j)\begin{document}$r_{\text {burst }}^{(j)}$\end{document} is the peak burst firing rate, τburst(j)\begin{document}$\tau_{\text {burst }}^{(j)}$\end{document} is the peak width, t(j) is the expected burst time, and δburst(j)\begin{document}$\delta_{\text {burst }}^{(j)}$\end{document} is the jittering for the burst peak. t(j) is defined as t(j)=ton(j)−Tsong(i−1)/(NH−1)\begin{document}$t^{(j)}=t_{o n}^{(j)}-T_{\mathrm{song}}(i-1) /\left(N_{H}-1\right)$\end{document}, where ton(j)\begin{document}$t_{o n}^{(j)}$\end{document} is the song onset, Tsong is the song length. Notice that rburst(j),τburst(j)\begin{document}$r_{\text {burst }}^{(j)}, \tau_{\text {burst }}^{(j)}$\end{document}, and δburst(j)\begin{document}$\delta_{\text {burst }}^{(j)}$\end{document} can vary over renditions.

We also tested the models using developing, progressively regular, premotor bursting profiles in Fig. S2. In these cases, rburst(j)\begin{document}$r_{\text {burst }}^{(j)}$\end{document} follows a log-normal distribution with mean 150 Hz and standard deviation 70ψ(j) Hz, (τburst(j)−20)\begin{document}$\left(\tau_{\text {burst }}^{(j)}-20\right)$\end{document} follows an exponential distribution with scale parameter 60ψ(j) ms, and δburst(j)∼N(0,100ψ(j))ms\begin{document}$\delta_{\text {burst }}^{(j)} \sim \mathcal{N}(0,100 \psi(j)) \mathrm{ms}$\end{document}, where
ψ(j)=exp⁡(−3j/Nrend) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \psi(j)=\exp \left(-3 j / N_{\text {rend }}\right)$$\end{document}
is a scaling factor decreasing smoothly over training.

To quantify the changes of error landscape in Fig. 5F, we directly perturbed the sparse embedding of the tutor song input because the alternative perturbation approach described above leads to great variance in the effective difference between perturbed and correct tutor song patterns for each choice of perturbation strength (Fig. S1G). First, a noise pattern, 𝐲~\begin{document}$$\tilde{\mathbf{y}}$$\end{document} is generated by randomly shuffling the elements of the sparse embedding of the syllable to be perturbed. Given a perturbation strength variable γ, the new perturbed pattern, ŷ(γ) was calculated as
y^(γ)=1−γ2y+γy~. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \hat{\mathbf{y}}(\gamma)=\sqrt{1-\gamma^{2} \mathbf{y}+\gamma \tilde{\mathbf{y}} .}$$\end{document}
For Fig. 5G, we simply scaled the sparse embedding by a scaling parameter a: ŷ(a) = ay, where a = 0 corresponds to the deafening case and a = 1 corresponds to the correct singing case.
